EP. Group Cleans Up Cemetery
A long, hot day in the sun spent cleaning up someone else’s trash is the contribution
made by the Young Marines Saturday to help beautify El Paso.
The First Platoon of the Dean Hawkins Detachment, composed of boys from 8 to 17 years of age, turned outina clean-up detail to tidy the area east of Concordia Cemetery,
Andy Cohen, president of the Beautify El Paso Assn., said,
“THAT PLACE was a disgrace," Mr. Cohen said. “I don’t know whose responsibility it is to maintain the area,
but I should think families whose loved ones are buried there would be concerned.”
The Young Marines expect to take the next several weeks to get the trash and weeds
cleaned up. ‘‘The Beautify El Paso Assn. is going to do everything in its power to help these young men clean it up,”’
Mr. Cohen said. The lots are filled with weeds, bushes, barbed wire, broken bottles, cans and paper. “I didn’t realize how awful it was until I took a look at it.’”
The Young Marines, a drill organization headed by ExMarine Ralph Orner, provided their own tools and lunches for the day-long task.
Mr. Cohen appealed to other groups of young people to adopt similarly abused city areas for special clean-up projects. “‘We need to educate our citizens to do something about
careless litter besides criticizing it, as well as make them conscious about disposing of trash in proper receptacles. Pollution has to be stopped. . . whether it’s in the air, on the ground or in our minds.”’

