Members of El Paso Sports Association Inc., are keeping their fingers crossed this year. This is El Paso’s sixth year back in organized baseball and the club underwriters haven’t
had to bail out the ball club yet. .
El Paso is one of the few remaining clubs in the country owned and operated. “by the community on a non-profit basis.
The unique El Paso Sports Association consists of some 40 local business and professional men who underwrite the baseball operation.
///   Two Texas millionaires, Tommy Mercer and Lamar Hunt own the Dallas-Fort Worth Spurs, while the St. Louis Cards
set up the management for the Little Rock Travelers.
This leaves El Paso pretty much with local community ownership and management.
The 40 members of El Paso Sports Association have been a solid foundation for the ball club with very little change in
the membership since it was first formed.
Some members hion voluntered to put in additional time. John Phelan heads up the group
as president and is the ram-rod behind the local baseball operation.
Two real working vice presidents are Harry Elliott and Andy Cohen. Elliott has supervised the concession operation
since the ball club started up again, and Andy Cohen has been a constant source of help on players.
El Paso started out in the class D Sophmore League in 1961. In 1962 they jumped to the Texas League and won the pennant at the same time.
The 1963 team also aroused a lot of excitement as they went on to break the Texas League home run record for a season.

