Members of the Beautify El Paso Association and their guests were shown results of the Dickshire program to rid
the City’s streets and scenic views of aluminum beer cans.
Dickshire, distributor of Coors beer, is paying one-half cent a can for any aluminum beer can turned in. Thus far,
the El Paso firm has paid out $8,118.60 for the return of 1,867,278 cans.
Cans may be turned in to Dickshire, 203 Chelsea, anytime between noon and 3 p.m. on Tuesdays end Thursdays.
Richard Azar, Dickshire president, said that currently about 30 per cent of the total number of cans handled by his
firm are being returned. He said that the history of the program shows that by September about 50 per cent of the
cans will be brought in.
The cans are baled and then re-processed for future use.
Presiding over the meeting was Andy Cohen, Dickshire sales manager and chairman of the Beautify El Paso Asso-
ciation. Special guests included State Rep.-Elect Charlie Tupper Jr., Democratic nominee to the State House of Rep-
resentatives Jim Kaster IJr., Robert W. ‘‘Pete” Lee, editor of the Herald-Post, and Dorrance H. Guy, executive editor of The El Paso Times.
